A||||16||sjp777||Dirty rotten lying gas gauge||||8||
Z||000000||sjp777||05-13-2001||07:34 PM||||Ran out of gas today. Gauge showed 1/4 tank. When I fill it, needle goes to way above full and stays there for 2 days. Evidently the range is a little off. Gee ya think?<P>Any way to fix this short of pulling the tank? Anybody try taking the needle off and putting it back on a little retarded from original postion?<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||8
Z||000001||TOM||05-13-2001||07:56 PM||||Mine did that awhile back. I ran out pulling into work. After replacing the module and the coil. Becaue I know it had gas. I put in a gallon and it fired right up. So I left it for awhile. Just knowint that 1/4 meant empty. Then one day it just started reading right again. I have no idea what it was or if it will come back.||||reg||1
Z||000002||p4n1c||05-13-2001||08:31 PM||||The probe could be out of whack in the fuel tank.  When dad put the new fuel tank on my car it did a lot of screwy things, it seems fine now, but it's usually a bit off.<P>I went 200kms in the Cutlass and it still read full then started dropping every 10 minutes until it found the correct level.<P>Another time the low fuel light started flashing(this is a bad thing, you're fine when it's solid, but when it's flashing and all the fuel bars are gone, you're in trouble), I went another 20km's on it.  Got home, fired it up the next morning and I had a 1/4 tank back.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||05-13-2001||09:45 PM||||Very common problem.  Has to do mainly with the sending unit inside the tank.  Though I havent dropped a tank myself, I am planning on doing it sometime this summer, and doing the fix described at this link:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=w6x2v73u&Catid=22&Id=46&Action=Edit" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=w6x2v73u&Catid=22&Id=46&Action=Edit</A> <P>I know that my gauge is correctly calibrated, I did the resistor test and adjusted the needle accordingly.  The hardest part, it seems, in the fix above is going to be dropping the tank.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000004||ray b||05-14-2001||12:34 AM||||just move the needle to the full mark [when the tank is full] one drop of glue real cheap fix <BR>btw the in tank sending unit is real high priced at dealers if you want one go to a junkyard. but if it moves it is good just out of position pointer not sender failure<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||05-14-2001||01:02 AM||||I wouldnt move and glue the needle. As I said now mine works like it should. If I glued it then it would be off the other direction and stuck there||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||05-14-2001||08:56 AM||||I have never seen or driven a 4-cylinder Fiero that didn't have this problem.  It sucks, but just think of the 1/4-tank line as "E" and you'll be fine.<P>The gas gauge on every V6 Fiero I've ever driven worked just fine.  What gives?||||reg||8
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||05-14-2001||09:05 AM||||Ive notice the needle on my V6 85 SE seems to move in 1/4 tank increments. I'll think, gee Im getting great mileage now, and then the needle will drop 1/4, or ill turn a corner and it will drop and a few minutes it will jump back up.<P>  Since my car sat for 2 years I suspect the sender is gummed up or rusty.<P>  Always use your trip odo to keep track of your mileage, and as a backup fuel gauge.||||reg||1
Z||000008||p4n1c||05-14-2001||09:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>Ive notice the needle on my V6 85 SE seems to move in 1/4 tank increments. I'll think, gee Im getting great mileage now, and then the needle will drop 1/4, or ill turn a corner and it will drop and a few minutes it will jump back up.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My friends Sunbird(it was a really old one) did this, whenever he would corner a little to hard the gas guage would drop to zero and the car would stall.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>I still say the intank sender is out of whack.  I can't see it being the gauge.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International||||reg||1
Z||000009||Steve Normington||05-14-2001||10:38 AM||||Both my first Fiero (84 SC) and my current (85 SE V-6) have the opposite problem. They'll both indicate 1/4 tank when I have half a tank left. I've driven as far as 50 miles on a "empty" tank according to the gage.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cheever2||05-14-2001||11:01 AM||||Both my 88's, 4-cyl and 6-cyl, are empty when it says 1/4.<P>The GT was driven once a month around the block, but I'm driving it again after 19 months.  Now I find that once the gauge gets down to below 1/2, it will jump way up above full, as far as the needle will go.  When I fill it up again, it goes back to the "normal" of just a little above full.  Crazy.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Old Lar||05-14-2001||11:32 AM||||200 miles ofdriving means fill the tank.  I have an 88 that takes 10 gallons after you run out.  Something isn't right.  Drop the tank... and fix it, when I get real ambitious.  That is what the trip odometer is for.||||reg||1
Z||000012||cadaver||05-14-2001||01:40 PM||||Yeah except my trip odometer is no bueno.||||reg||1
Z||000013||LZeitgeist||05-14-2001||05:07 PM||||My '88 4-cyl. was always fine, but when the needle hit 'E', it was dry. No room for error.<P>My '88 6-cyl. is 1/4 tank off - when full, it reads past the top of the gauge; when empty, it reads 1/4 tank. I hate that crap... *sigh*||||reg||1
Z||000014||ray b||05-14-2001||05:41 PM||||if the pointer moves the sender is good!!!<BR>the pointer can move on the shaft just move it back so it reads right level of the tank<BR>and glue it in place.comon problem on 88's both of mine have had it the pointer is not atached to the shaft it is stuck on and over time gets moved to a wrong reading. <BR>JUST MOVE IT BACK <P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000015||trigger||05-14-2001||06:13 PM||||I have an 88 4cyl auto and my gauge is fine.  I have never run out but I've had it close enought to the red to get really nervous.  I know if I completely top it off the needle goes a little past the full, but other than that it seems fine.<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Trigger.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||sjp777||05-14-2001||08:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>I have never seen or driven a 4-cylinder Fiero that didn't have this problem.  It sucks, but just think of the 1/4-tank line as "E" and you'll be fine.<P>The gas gauge on every V6 Fiero I've ever driven worked just fine.  What gives?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It is a V6, as was my 86. They both had this problem although the 88's is worse<BR><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
